WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:08.378
This course is a detailed introduction to system design for software developers and engineers. Gaurav Sen developed this course.

00:00:08.528 --> 00:00:12.960
He is an experienced software engineer and he also has a popular YouTube channel.

00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:19.467
You will learn about basic engineering design patterns that are used to build large-scale distributed systems.

00:00:19.544 --> 00:00:23.976
In the second part of the course you will learn how to use the principles from the first part 

00:00:23.976 --> 00:00:27.150
to design and code a live streaming video app.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:27.810
Hello everyone.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.430
Welcome to the System Design course.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.270
This course is for beginners in a
sense that if you have never done

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.085
system design before or you have done
very little system design or you have

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.620
just heard of it or read about it,
but not got an opportunity to actually

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.480
do it, this course is right for you.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.170
At the end of this course, you'll be
able to identify some basic engineering

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:50.100
design patterns, which are used to
design large scale distributed systems.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.730
Let me define each of
these terms in detail.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.430
Large scale distributed systems,
as the first part of the term, says

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:04.650
large scale, meaning that something
which is being used a lot or is very

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:10.110
intensive in terms of compute or data
or any, computer engineering principle.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.160
For example Google, let's say Google Maps.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.400
This is large scale because
A: it has a lot of data.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.970
The whole world's map has
to be stored, incited.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.260
B: it's being used by a lot of people.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.930
C: it's being updated very
frequently, and b, it has a lot of

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.400
performance expectations from it.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.380
You don't expect Google Maps to go down.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.170
You expect Google Maps to
return results quickly.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.690
You expect it to be
accurate, so on and so forth.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.960
So it's a very good product
being used at a large scale.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:47.680
Distributed systems means that the server
or the code that is actually executing

00:01:47.685 --> 00:01:49.840
this program is not in one place.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:51.520
It's disputed all around the world.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.630
So you might have one server in
India, one server in the us, one in

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:00.040
Japan for tolerance, so that if one
of these servers crashes, the rest

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.085
of the servers can take the load.

00:02:01.085 --> 00:02:04.270
It's also for performance.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:08.200
If the Indians want a Google Map result,
they go and talk to the Indian server

00:02:08.205 --> 00:02:09.910
because that gives a quick result back.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:14.350
While if they go to the US server, they
have to go across continents and then

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.450
get a response, which is a slow result.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:17.740
Okay?

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.750
So that's the point of a large
scale distributed system.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.620
Many of these companies which
provide these solutions, Expect

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.800
their engineers to know about system
design, to know how to build these

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.120
large scale distributed systems.

00:02:28.539 --> 00:02:32.199
So to do this, their engineering
team depends on design patterns,

00:02:32.204 --> 00:02:33.160
which we mentioned earlier.

00:02:33.669 --> 00:02:39.010
Design patterns are particular practices,
principles, or processes which are used

00:02:39.015 --> 00:02:41.470
by engineers to build these systems.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:47.290
For example, you have a common
problem of a celebrity posting on

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:51.580
social media and that post being
distributed to a lot of their followers.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.840
For example, if Brad Pitt posts
something on YouTube and then post on

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.940
LinkedIn, the problem is very similar.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:03.520
It's one piece of content which has
to be made into an event and notified

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.960
to millions of people potentially.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.340
And what you want to do is you want to
notify them quickly, but you also don't

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:12.280
want to put too much load on your service
so that the rest of the requests, which

00:03:12.285 --> 00:03:14.200
are coming into it continue being served.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:19.420
This is a common problem, so you
extract out a common problem and you

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:20.830
solve it using a common solution.

00:03:21.965 --> 00:03:22.990
, this would be a design pattern.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:27.370
Right now, you don't know what design
patterns exist, but one popular design

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.050
pattern for this kind of a problem is
a publisher subscriber model where Brad

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:36.550
Pit is a publisher and their event is
subscribed to by millions of people.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.250
And Intermediately decides the pace at
which you send these notifications, which

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:46.210
keeps the server loaded low, and also
make sure that all the notifications

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:47.590
actually reach all the subscribers.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.190
Now, seeing this, you can tell that as an
engineer you can take a lot of business

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:54.730
requirements and convert them into
technical solutions, and engineers use

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.570
system design patterns to make reliable,
scalable, and maintainable systems.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.620
This helps them convert business
requirements into technical solutions.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.530
If this sounds exciting to you,
watch's the rest of the video

00:04:05.535 --> 00:04:06.670
and you won't be disappointed.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.140
Okay, let's start with an example.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:21.580
Let's say that you join a company
which does broadcasting of

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:23.590
videos to millions of people.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.970
This could be Hot Star, it could
be YouTube, it could be Zoom also.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.590
So some sort of events which are
being broadcast to millions of people.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:38.650
And you as an engineer have to devise
the solution for this, come up with some

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.540
technology which can handle this problem.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.290
So the first thing that we need to
do as engineers is to define these

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:46.990
requirements from the user's perspective.

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.250
And often engineers don't do this.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:54.750
They're product managers and a company
which write a product requirement

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.510
document based on user feedback and data.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:00.310
So that's good to have.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:07.840
You have a well documented, thought
business banked document, which the

00:05:07.845 --> 00:05:11.380
engineer can then read and decide
on how they can make it happen.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:14.560
Amongst these, you pick up the
most important features first.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.060
Taking the example of a live streaming
system, users who tune into your system

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.710
should be able to watch the stream live.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.780
Whether you see it at HD quality or you
see it at four 80 p, that's probably

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:30.010
a secondary issue, but being able
to see the video is a primary issue.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.370
So for that, you have to make sure
that your server doesn't go down, and

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:36.100
you have to also make sure that your
bandwidth requirements are sufficient.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.600
Second, you reduce these
features to data definitions.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:45.760
So one of the features which might be
defined is users should be able to like a

00:05:45.765 --> 00:05:47.500
video or should be able to like a comment.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:52.560
What you then do is you look at
the concept of a, like the abstract

00:05:52.565 --> 00:05:54.430
concept of somebody liking something.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:55.390
What does that mean?

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:59.530
It means that a particular user
likes a particular comment.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:03.190
Okay, so it has a like id, it
has a user ID who's done this.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:04.180
It has a timestamp.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.760
When was this video liked or
when was this comment liked?

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:08.830
The comment itself is an abstract concept.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.870
It's probably going to
be mapped into an object.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.860
It has an id, it has a user
who has typed this comment.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:19.630
It has a creation time and maybe it also
has a thread in which it was posted.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:24.820
So you're seeing that I'm taking features
or I'm taking these abstract concepts

00:06:24.825 --> 00:06:28.840
from the product requirement document and
converting them into data definitions,

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:30.650
which are used, useful for an engineer.

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:34.000
These definitions can then be
mapped into objects, which can

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.310
then be mapped into the database.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.820
Once you've defined the data that
you need to store, you need to

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:44.110
define endpoints through which this
data can be manipulated or queried.

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:52.580
So I want to read comments, give me an
API and sgp api ftp API for all WeCare.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.630
The network protocol at this point
is not important, but some method

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:59.320
by which I can send an electronic
signal from one place to our server,

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:02.710
and our server can respond to that
signal with the data that they need.

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:07.150
So our server is now encapsulating
this data as per the user

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:10.720
requirement and defining some
endpoints, which are called APIs.

00:07:11.785 --> 00:07:14.305
So that external users can
query and manipulate our data.

00:07:14.665 --> 00:07:19.285
We do this for every feature that
we have, core features and also

00:07:19.585 --> 00:07:21.115
optional or good to have features.

00:07:21.475 --> 00:07:24.235
Usually a product requirement
document does not define optional

00:07:24.235 --> 00:07:25.405
or good to have features.

00:07:25.495 --> 00:07:28.865
These are all core features which
are required in the document.

00:07:29.185 --> 00:07:31.525
The good to have features will
be probably picked up in the next

00:07:31.525 --> 00:07:35.915
document, so you don't need to think
of which features are optional,

00:07:36.055 --> 00:07:38.995
but you do think of which features
are most important as an engineer.

00:07:40.315 --> 00:07:44.695
The second thing is you also have
certain engineering requirements.

00:07:44.725 --> 00:07:49.195
When you're creating these designs or
coming up some code, you want to make

00:07:49.195 --> 00:07:54.865
sure that none of your services fail
If there is an outage, so for example,

00:07:54.865 --> 00:07:59.215
if you have an outage in India and your
common service is in India, then you

00:07:59.215 --> 00:08:01.015
don't want the entire system to collapse.

00:08:01.165 --> 00:08:04.165
We talked about this a little
earlier, that you have multiple

00:08:04.165 --> 00:08:07.645
server spread across the world to
avoid a single point of failure.

00:08:08.965 --> 00:08:11.665
. You may also have multiple
servers in India itself.

00:08:12.195 --> 00:08:16.855
So if one Indian server fails, another
one picks up its responsibility.

00:08:17.905 --> 00:08:20.785
So here you might have some sort
of data duplication because of

00:08:20.785 --> 00:08:23.455
which the other server is quickly
able to pick up the responsibility.

00:08:23.935 --> 00:08:26.965
Or you have some sort of partitioning
that 50% of the users are going

00:08:26.965 --> 00:08:30.085
to this server, 50% of the users
are going to another server.

00:08:30.355 --> 00:08:33.835
So just 50% of the users are affected
under the common and often missed

00:08:33.835 --> 00:08:37.585
out engineering requirement is
extensibility, which means it's not

00:08:37.585 --> 00:08:42.355
just about the technical solution
that you come up with, but also how

00:08:42.535 --> 00:08:44.125
easy it is to change that solution.

00:08:45.325 --> 00:08:50.575
For example, if you write code to
send a message to millions of users,

00:08:50.965 --> 00:08:54.145
and you need to make a small tweak to
that code because now you don't want

00:08:54.145 --> 00:08:55.765
to just send it to 1 million users.

00:08:55.825 --> 00:08:59.034
You also want to check whether
they have read those messages.

00:08:59.365 --> 00:08:59.784
So read

00:08:59.784 --> 00:09:00.678
receipt.

00:09:00.678 --> 00:09:02.465
The problem

00:09:02.515 --> 00:09:05.485
with writing code, which is
highly coupled with the feature.

00:09:05.875 --> 00:09:09.025
Is that whenever there's a changing
requirement, you have to put in a

00:09:09.025 --> 00:09:14.125
lot of effort to redesign, test,
and actually deploy that code again.

00:09:15.145 --> 00:09:17.724
So you want your features,
your engineering features.

00:09:17.785 --> 00:09:20.755
To be extensible for this, you
have to take out your engineering

00:09:20.755 --> 00:09:22.584
pistol ball and gaze deeply

00:09:22.584 --> 00:09:23.305
into it.

00:09:23.305 --> 00:09:24.025
Actually what

00:09:24.025 --> 00:09:27.505
you do is, as an experienced
engineer, especially, you look back at

00:09:27.505 --> 00:09:30.415
your past projects you look

00:09:30.415 --> 00:09:33.474
back at things that you have done,
you look back at things that people

00:09:33.474 --> 00:09:34.170
have done.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.214
You know what

00:09:35.214 --> 00:09:35.995
the world is doing.

00:09:36.265 --> 00:09:41.064
And based on that combined knowledge,
you build a system so that you can

00:09:42.145 --> 00:09:46.464
reasonably expect it to scale and
extend as requirements change and as

00:09:46.464 --> 00:09:49.974
more and more users join you, finally
this design needs to be tested.

00:09:50.094 --> 00:09:52.375
This is an important part
of system design, which is

00:09:52.375 --> 00:09:55.265
not really thought through

00:09:55.314 --> 00:09:58.435
in interviews, but in the real
world, when you have a system

00:09:59.035 --> 00:10:00.474
which is designed by, let's say a

00:10:00.474 --> 00:10:02.454
senior engineer that design

00:10:02.454 --> 00:10:03.875
has to
be tested.

00:10:03.875 --> 00:10:05.690
You run through

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:08.170
a couple of requests with edge

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:12.820
cases, with common
cases, and whether these

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:16.210
requests are having a sensible
flow in the system at a high level.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:21.280
The other thing that you can do is you
can use sophisticated tools to load test

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.072
this design.

00:10:22.072 --> 00:10:23.260
You can have

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:26.740
some sort of capacity estimation
to actually see whether this design

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:30.520
is feasible, but importantly, you
have to test this design before

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:32.410
you start getting into the code.

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:34.780
Okay.
Let's recap with an example.

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:37.030
Let's say we have a live streaming system.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:39.160
How would we go about designing this?

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:45.400
Requirements would be streaming
video, processing video, sending

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:47.520
video to multiple customers.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.570
So that is
broadcasting not failing

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:51.550
is a requirement.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:56.020
Showing advertisements, allowing
reactions, showing disclaimers

00:10:56.020 --> 00:11:00.040
or news flashes, having graceful
degradation of video quality

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:03.160
in case you have low bandwidth,
allowing multiple device support.

00:11:03.895 --> 00:11:04.435
and so on.

00:11:04.675 --> 00:11:07.194
Amongst these, you'll see that
the product requirements are

00:11:07.194 --> 00:11:08.484
mainly showing video to a lot

00:11:08.484 --> 00:11:09.528
of users.

00:11:09.528 --> 00:11:11.094
That's a major

00:11:11.094 --> 00:11:11.454
requirement.

00:11:11.454 --> 00:11:14.665
And also, of course, comments is a thing
and being able to react as a thing.

00:11:14.785 --> 00:11:17.935
You may also consider product requirements
to be showing a banner in case there's a

00:11:17.935 --> 00:11:18.564
problem.

00:11:18.564 --> 00:11:22.344
It is, but the core requirement,

00:11:22.344 --> 00:11:25.344
of course, is just showing a video,
so I'll pick this feature first.

00:11:25.435 --> 00:11:30.354
This means that I have to capture
video from a source, let's say, which

00:11:30.354 --> 00:11:34.584
is shooting at eight K, and I should
be able to store it in someplace

00:11:35.574 --> 00:11:40.254
in my server so that I can query it
later in a live streaming system.

00:11:40.854 --> 00:11:46.675
The later part, the query it later
is probably milliseconds, so I may

00:11:46.675 --> 00:11:48.234
not want to query that data at all.

00:11:48.714 --> 00:11:53.694
I may want to directly stream it
from the video camera onto millions

00:11:53.694 --> 00:11:55.555
of people taking a step back.

00:11:55.584 --> 00:12:00.144
It looks impractical because if I'm
shooting at eight k at very high

00:12:00.144 --> 00:12:02.319
quality,  and this

00:12:02.319 --> 00:12:02.860
is raw

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:03.580
footage.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:05.020
Sending that

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:08.410
much data to people on their
mobile phones is unreasonable.

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:13.750
So yes, I need to store it in some
sort of a database or a file system,

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:18.189
and then I should be able to stream
that or query that out so that I can

00:12:18.189 --> 00:12:20.170
distribute it to all of my customers.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:23.680
But I don't want my customers
to know exactly how I'm

00:12:23.685 --> 00:12:24.880
doing this or I don't

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.699
want them to know about, there's a
change in implementation tomorrow.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:30.579
I want this to be a black
box so that they can pay me

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:31.800
and relax.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:32.800
They just

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:34.660
hit an API and their problem is solved.

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:38.770
Of course, I might give them
clients which are download on the

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:40.240
app store which take

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.380
care of all of this API and querying
also so that as end users, they can

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:48.280
just enjoy watching an event instead
of thinking about how the technical

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:49.540
part of this is actually happening.

00:12:50.079 --> 00:12:52.060
These APIs have well defined signatures.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:53.740
You can tell that if I want

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:56.229
this video Id in a particular

00:12:56.229 --> 00:12:59.920
format, then I have to query a
particular API called Get video.

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:02.830
, which is going to be
returning me objects of

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:03.715
type frames.

00:13:03.715 --> 00:13:04.600
And those

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:05.770
frames are also well defined.

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:10.740
So if you have ever written a program,
you know that these API signatures

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:12.190
are very similar to method signatures.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:16.930
The only difference being that these APIs
might be queried not through a programming

00:13:16.935 --> 00:13:23.320
language, but through a network protocol
like G R P C, htp, ftp, any kind of

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:27.700
protocol which defines exactly how an
electronic message is going to be taken

00:13:27.705 --> 00:13:29.140
from one place and sent to another.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.870
And also how the response is
going to come back and how the

00:13:31.875 --> 00:13:33.790
behavior of this interaction is

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:35.205
going to be that is defined

00:13:35.205 --> 00:13:35.860
by the protocol.

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:36.820
So this

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:37.420
is great.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:38.320
We have a

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:41.290
system which is storing some
data, which is valuable to us

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:42.640
because we want to watch this

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:44.186
event live.

00:13:44.186 --> 00:13:46.430
And this data

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:47.890
is going to be queried using APIs.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:50.800
Also, these APIs are going
to be tested beforehand

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:52.630
so that, the clients

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:55.480
who are using this work, paying for
this service are not disappointed

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:56.380
when they actually query it.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:59.470
As engineers, we have to think
of various failures and our use.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:03.220
. What if the database, which is
stored in your videos crashes?

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:07.900
What if a particular firewall
on the internet starts

00:14:07.905 --> 00:14:09.280
blocking all of your requests?

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:12.370
What if one of the services that
you've written in your entire

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:17.050
system, one program, one piece of
code, starts misbehaving because

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:18.730
you have, introduced

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:19.300
a bug in it?

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:22.480
Or there is somebody who has
maliciously entered the system and

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:24.280
changed the code in that system?

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:26.770
We have to use some design principles
that we talked about earlier.

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:31.830
This challenge may also be a feature
request, like if you want the musician

00:14:31.835 --> 00:14:35.740
who's playing in this live event to
be able to talk to the audience, do

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:39.370
a back and forth with some audience
members who they select either

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:41.170
randomly or based on their activity.

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:49.390
So you have to display those users to this
musician live and be able to broadcast two

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.430
parties to millions of people out there.

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:55.210
So taking these requirements, let's try
to design a live streaming application.

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:56.680
So there's two ways to approach this.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:02.065
One is, From customers or our
clients to our server, which is

00:15:02.065 --> 00:15:03.145
out there in different parts of

00:15:03.145 --> 00:15:04.045
the world.

00:15:04.045 --> 00:15:05.395
And then to

00:15:05.395 --> 00:15:08.995
our database, which may be, again,
in different parts of the world.

00:15:10.645 --> 00:15:11.575
That's one way to think.

00:15:12.115 --> 00:15:16.285
And the other way to think is
from our database to our server.

00:15:16.465 --> 00:15:19.615
So what kind of detail do I need
to store to enable my server?

00:15:20.305 --> 00:15:24.145
And what kind of APIs do I
need to expose to enable my

00:15:24.515 --> 00:15:25.375
customers to be able to use my

00:15:25.375 --> 00:15:25.999
product?

00:15:25.999 --> 00:15:26.623
Okay.

00:15:26.623 --> 00:15:28.495
Both of these

00:15:28.495 --> 00:15:29.335
approaches are fine.

00:15:29.845 --> 00:15:31.885
They require different
ways of thinking, though.

00:15:32.935 --> 00:15:37.855
When it comes to a data based approach,
you will need to consider what

00:15:37.855 --> 00:15:39.325
kind of data do you need to store.

00:15:39.895 --> 00:15:42.355
And often you'll be thinking of these

00:15:43.235 --> 00:15:46.655
pieces of data as tables.

00:15:46.705 --> 00:15:48.295
So you need to store a video

00:15:48.295 --> 00:15:49.522
with an id.

00:15:49.522 --> 00:15:50.340
The video

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:54.105
has a name, it has a size,
and it has some data.

00:15:55.045 --> 00:15:55.915
That's one way to think.

00:15:56.935 --> 00:16:02.214
I prefer the other approach where
customers define their problems, which are

00:16:02.214 --> 00:16:09.115
then fulfilled using APIs on the server,
which are then fulfilled by storing

00:16:09.175 --> 00:16:12.115
some sort of data in the server, in the

00:16:12.115 --> 00:16:12.810
database.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:14.895
And that data

00:16:14.905 --> 00:16:15.865
is then mapped onto

00:16:15.865 --> 00:16:17.814
tables like and so for

00:16:17.814 --> 00:16:20.245
this system, this is the
product we'll be using.

00:16:21.865 --> 00:16:22.045
Okay?

00:16:22.045 --> 00:16:27.655
In our case, customers are live streaming
customers, so they may be streaming from

00:16:27.655 --> 00:16:35.214
their cell phones or laptops or the tv and
we can't assume which device is being used

00:16:35.214 --> 00:16:36.024
more often.

00:16:36.024 --> 00:16:36.834
In certain

00:16:36.834 --> 00:16:38.485
countries you'll have
certain devices being

00:16:38.485 --> 00:16:39.732
used more.

00:16:39.732 --> 00:16:40.979
There, there

00:16:40.979 --> 00:16:43.435
could also be a tablet of course,
from which they're streaming this

00:16:43.439 --> 00:16:43.798
video.

00:16:43.798 --> 00:16:44.875
But that there

00:16:44.875 --> 00:16:46.464
are multiple devices which need to be

00:16:46.464 --> 00:16:47.316
catered to.

00:16:47.316 --> 00:16:48.594
This is a

00:16:48.594 --> 00:16:50.995
front end UI design problem.

00:16:51.775 --> 00:16:55.285
System design is more to do
with the distributed systems'

00:16:55.285 --> 00:16:56.635
backend part of things.

00:16:57.714 --> 00:17:02.334
There is some system design involved when
it comes to API interactions and how to

00:17:02.334 --> 00:17:07.045
store or cash data on the front end, but
that is not what we are focusing on here.

00:17:07.194 --> 00:17:09.655
We are focusing on the
backend part of the system.

00:17:09.925 --> 00:17:10.425
Okay?

00:17:10.645 --> 00:17:15.415
So these clients need to be able
to query our server in real time.

00:17:15.474 --> 00:17:17.485
So what does our server need to have?

00:17:17.694 --> 00:17:22.015
Its APIs are going to be something like
get video and you pass in the video

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:24.895
ID and you pass in your device type.

00:17:25.885 --> 00:17:29.395
So I send you the resolution based
on your device type and you might

00:17:29.395 --> 00:17:32.215
have a particular offset that
you know, I've already seen the

00:17:32.215 --> 00:17:33.565
first 10 minutes of the video.

00:17:33.895 --> 00:17:35.994
Show me everything after the 10th minute.

00:17:36.474 --> 00:17:40.974
If I have a video API like this,
I also need to return something.

00:17:41.275 --> 00:17:42.654
So the return type could.

00:17:43.869 --> 00:17:45.040
Some frames, some

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:46.004
video frames.

00:17:46.004 --> 00:17:46.970
Let's say

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:49.300
each of these frames is 10 seconds long.

00:17:49.305 --> 00:17:52.480
So a single frame is what I
sent back, such that it is off

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:54.369
this video for this

00:17:54.369 --> 00:17:55.060
device.

00:17:55.690 --> 00:18:00.700
And after 10 minutes, so 10 minutes to
10 minutes and 10 seconds, that frame

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.410
is picked up and sent to the client.

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:03.580
If you know about

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:05.410
API design, that this

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:06.790
API is not

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:07.842
well named.

00:18:07.842 --> 00:18:08.865
Get video

00:18:08.865 --> 00:18:11.620
means you get an entire video,
but you're returning a frame.

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:15.460
So maybe we can rename
this to get video frame.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:17.080
Other thing is if you're using a

00:18:17.085 --> 00:18:20.620
REST protocol get is going

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:22.149
to be defined in the request itself.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:23.020
So g e

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:25.780
t or here is going

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:30.370
to say that Get Me Video is
enough to say that you want

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:31.078
a video.

00:18:31.078 --> 00:18:32.139
In our case,

00:18:32.139 --> 00:18:35.560
like we said, it's a frame, so
maybe get frame or get video

00:18:35.565 --> 00:18:37.000
frame is what we are looking at.

00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:42.129
You notice that Wimu also has a similar
API when it comes to getting the next 10

00:18:42.129 --> 00:18:42.928
seconds.

00:18:42.928 --> 00:18:45.324
YouTube also has

00:18:45.324 --> 00:18:48.685
an api which tells you from which point to
which point do you want to see the video?

00:18:49.074 --> 00:18:50.245
I'll give you all those things.

00:18:50.635 --> 00:18:53.094
Great.
So we have this, what else?

00:18:53.635 --> 00:18:54.955
You should be able to
comment on the video.

00:18:55.614 --> 00:18:56.114
Okay.

00:18:56.364 --> 00:19:00.054
So what you should be able to do is
you should be able to post a comment.

00:19:00.705 --> 00:19:01.264
Again,

00:19:01.324 --> 00:19:03.145
I'm using well-known

00:19:03.145 --> 00:19:08.695
concept in rest post means you want
to put something in the server, you

00:19:08.695 --> 00:19:11.455
want to manipulate some data, you
want to add some data to the server.

00:19:12.445 --> 00:19:17.455
So if I say post a comment with an ID
here, I don't care about which device

00:19:17.459 --> 00:19:20.814
it is coming from because the comment is
just data, which has to be persisted in

00:19:20.814 --> 00:19:21.475
the server.

00:19:21.475 --> 00:19:22.465
Based on that,

00:19:22.465 --> 00:19:24.175
I'm not
going to be, changing my

00:19:24.175 --> 00:19:26.754
response or changing the behavior
in which I'm posting the comment.

00:19:27.235 --> 00:19:28.225
So I don't need that.

00:19:28.284 --> 00:19:30.745
I don't need an offset, but
I do need the comment data.

00:19:31.584 --> 00:19:32.754
I need the author of the comment.

00:19:33.715 --> 00:19:35.935
I need the post on which
this comment was made.

00:19:35.995 --> 00:19:38.304
So maybe the video on which
this comment was made.

00:19:38.304 --> 00:19:38.965
So the video ID.

00:19:40.764 --> 00:19:46.734
And similarly for each requirement, we
can expose APIs, which will allow us to

00:19:46.794 --> 00:19:48.864
query and manipulate the data as we want.

00:19:49.254 --> 00:19:53.754
So that roughly defines our
server side capabilities.

00:19:54.284 --> 00:19:55.494
Okay, we haven't spoken

00:19:55.494 --> 00:19:59.164
about anything in detail.

00:19:59.214 --> 00:20:01.914
We haven't talked about the network
protocol also that we use, but

00:20:02.274 --> 00:20:04.074
roughly this is what it's, okay.

00:20:04.254 --> 00:20:08.094
Now let's go for the next part,
which is the database side of things.

00:20:09.414 --> 00:20:12.624
What kind of data we need to
store to satisfy these APIs.

00:20:14.004 --> 00:20:15.744
Returning frames is
something we want to do.

00:20:16.194 --> 00:20:18.924
Storing comments is something we want
to do and also probably get those

00:20:18.924 --> 00:20:19.584
comments.

00:20:19.584 --> 00:20:22.224
And to get video

00:20:22.224 --> 00:20:25.014
frames, we also probably
need to put video frames.

00:20:25.554 --> 00:20:27.024
What kind of database should we use?

00:20:27.294 --> 00:20:28.374
Comments are rather simple.

00:20:28.374 --> 00:20:33.474
You can store this in an SQL database
such that an SQL table having an ID

00:20:33.604 --> 00:20:34.572
with you.

00:20:34.572 --> 00:20:36.024
The data of

00:20:36.024 --> 00:20:37.014
the comments are text.

00:20:38.125 --> 00:20:38.784
being stored as a

00:20:38.784 --> 00:20:39.478
problem.

00:20:39.478 --> 00:20:40.865
The author,

00:20:40.915 --> 00:20:43.975
which is a foreign key
to a user table, right?

00:20:43.975 --> 00:20:45.865
So in a user table, you

00:20:45.865 --> 00:20:47.155
have an id and this

00:20:47.155 --> 00:20:49.764
author is going to be
mapped to an ID over here.

00:20:50.125 --> 00:20:51.745
Along with that, we have a video id.

00:20:51.774 --> 00:20:56.485
So there's a video table also,
which has an ID and some data.

00:20:57.475 --> 00:21:01.585
The video IDs are going to
be mapped in this way, okay?

00:21:01.645 --> 00:21:06.024
An example of this would be
video ID 10 is over here.

00:21:06.445 --> 00:21:08.455
This is a cricket match.

00:21:08.725 --> 00:21:10.524
Video ID 11 would be a musical

00:21:10.524 --> 00:21:11.082
event.

00:21:11.082 --> 00:21:11.640
Okay?

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:13.314
And this is

00:21:13.314 --> 00:21:14.095
a comment table.

00:21:14.635 --> 00:21:20.784
So for this cricket match, a comment
was made in which they said, Hey,

00:21:21.175 --> 00:21:23.875
and the author was author number one.

00:21:23.935 --> 00:21:25.375
Author number one happens to be called.

00:21:26.905 --> 00:21:27.745
That's their username.

00:21:28.825 --> 00:21:34.959
So if you have to display all comments,
In the front end for a particular video.

00:21:35.139 --> 00:21:38.739
What you want to do is you want
to get all of the comments in

00:21:38.739 --> 00:21:41.589
this table for that video 10.

00:21:42.729 --> 00:21:45.489
Then you want to get the data for that
user because you want to display the

00:21:45.494 --> 00:21:47.409
username who has made that comment.

00:21:48.009 --> 00:21:52.179
And you also want to get some video data
because you have to display that on top

00:21:52.239 --> 00:21:53.889
of the page where the comment was made.

00:21:55.599 --> 00:21:58.059
So in this way, we are able
to satisfy our requirement of

00:21:58.869 --> 00:22:00.489
posting comments, posting video.

00:22:00.549 --> 00:22:02.139
Now, overall, this system is complete.

00:22:03.099 --> 00:22:03.639
We have a

00:22:03.639 --> 00:22:06.789
system which can answer

00:22:06.789 --> 00:22:07.419
queries.

00:22:07.869 --> 00:22:10.449
Okay?
This is good enough to

00:22:10.449 --> 00:22:11.304
start with.

00:22:11.304 --> 00:22:12.159
This diagram

00:22:12.159 --> 00:22:12.659
is not

00:22:12.709 --> 00:22:13.592
incomplete.

00:22:13.592 --> 00:22:16.239
It's very rough

00:22:16.239 --> 00:22:17.427
or
high level.

00:22:17.427 --> 00:22:18.399
There's nothing

00:22:18.399 --> 00:22:23.049
here which is concrete, nothing,
which is, let's say, useful

00:22:23.049 --> 00:22:24.279
when it comes to implementation.

00:22:24.909 --> 00:22:28.749
A at this point in time, we have a
rough idea of how we are going to be

00:22:28.749 --> 00:22:31.779
talking to each other, not what we
are going to use to make this happen.

00:22:32.589 --> 00:22:34.599
So let's get into those
implementation details.

00:22:35.619 --> 00:22:39.639
Firstly, on the client side, is
there something we need to do?

00:22:40.719 --> 00:22:45.369
Yes, different APIs require
different behaviors.

00:22:45.999 --> 00:22:51.249
Posting a comment means that I'm posting
this once and I'll be querying that

00:22:51.249 --> 00:22:54.909
comment maybe soon, but it's not like
I need something to keep happening.

00:22:54.909 --> 00:22:56.739
I don't need continuous
updates on the comment.

00:22:57.309 --> 00:23:01.689
Those notifications can be given
periodically to me, or maybe I

00:23:01.689 --> 00:23:04.899
don't need those notifications
at all after a few months.

00:23:04.959 --> 00:23:07.239
I just don't need
notifications on that comment.

00:23:08.649 --> 00:23:12.249
So you see over here, you
have non real time behavior.

00:23:13.749 --> 00:23:14.739
What about a video frame?

00:23:16.959 --> 00:23:21.399
When I ask for a video frame, I
usually need to ask for the next

00:23:21.399 --> 00:23:23.259
video frame immediately after that.

00:23:23.919 --> 00:23:27.219
So I'm watching a live video,
I ask for a video frame.

00:23:27.279 --> 00:23:29.469
I am sorted for the next 10 seconds.

00:23:29.769 --> 00:23:31.719
In five seconds, I'll be
asking the next video frame.

00:23:33.700 --> 00:23:34.750
. So that behavior is different.

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:36.160
It's a more continuous behavior.

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:37.480
Okay?

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:41.139
So maybe we need to use different
network protocols to make

00:23:41.139 --> 00:23:42.324
this happen.

00:23:42.324 --> 00:23:43.510
What would

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:44.470
I use for a comment?

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:50.560
I would use the most common
network protocol when it

00:23:50.565 --> 00:23:51.610
comes to disability systems,

00:23:51.615 --> 00:23:54.970
which is hdp, HTP gives

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:57.399
us the benefit that you
have a stateless server.

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:01.300
You don't need to store any information
when you're handling a request.

00:24:01.629 --> 00:24:03.730
A stateless server is basically, I

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:05.830
have no idea where you

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:07.570
are from or what you want.

00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:09.790
Define everything in the request.

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:10.620
Okay?

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:13.899
Goon wants the next 10 seconds of video.

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.420
What do you mean by the next 10 seconds?

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:17.980
Who is God of San?

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:23.410
Okay, so what should be there
in the request is here's Gossen.

00:24:23.950 --> 00:24:24.940
This is his user id.

00:24:24.940 --> 00:24:26.889
You can actually go and
look it up in your database.

00:24:28.495 --> 00:24:29.395
It's not the next 10

00:24:29.395 --> 00:24:30.955
seconds that he wants the

00:24:30.955 --> 00:24:33.264
video from minute number 10 to 10 minutes.

00:24:33.264 --> 00:24:34.045
10 seconds.

00:24:34.555 --> 00:24:34.885
Okay.

00:24:34.885 --> 00:24:36.595
That video length is also well defined.

00:24:37.075 --> 00:24:39.175
There is no concept of
the next point of view.

00:24:39.325 --> 00:24:41.185
And the video ID is mentioned.

00:24:41.455 --> 00:24:45.055
It's not like Gora wants the next 10
seconds of the video he was watching.

00:24:45.205 --> 00:24:46.855
No, I have no idea what we do.

00:24:46.855 --> 00:24:50.665
He was watching you define which
video needs to be pulled up here.

00:24:51.024 --> 00:24:54.355
You might think that this is an obvious
point, like what's so special about

00:24:55.105 --> 00:24:56.365
defining everything in the client

00:24:56.365 --> 00:24:56.816
itself.

00:24:56.816 --> 00:24:58.170
It looks a

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:00.955
little tedious and stupid, but doing it

00:25:00.955 --> 00:25:01.750
is fine.

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:02.545
Doesn't everyone

00:25:02.545 --> 00:25:03.045
do it?

00:25:03.955 --> 00:25:04.682
Not
necessarily.

00:25:04.682 --> 00:25:06.325
When you ask

00:25:06.325 --> 00:25:12.445
for the next 10 seconds of video, for
example, very often as a client, you don't

00:25:12.445 --> 00:25:14.875
know what the next 10 seconds should be.

00:25:15.895 --> 00:25:16.615
Take an example.

00:25:18.625 --> 00:25:22.105
Give me the video at 10 minutes, then 10.

00:25:22.105 --> 00:25:23.065
10 seconds from 10 minutes.

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:23.545
Okay?

00:25:23.545 --> 00:25:24.355
That's one chunk.

00:25:25.375 --> 00:25:26.665
Let's say you make that request.

00:25:27.175 --> 00:25:29.245
And the server is taking
time to give a response.

00:25:29.814 --> 00:25:32.814
Now you come back and you say,
give me the same video because

00:25:32.814 --> 00:25:34.135
you didn't receive it in time.

00:25:35.185 --> 00:25:38.875
The server is going to look
at that request and serve

00:25:38.875 --> 00:25:40.044
you the same response again.

00:25:40.645 --> 00:25:44.425
The other approach to this would
be the client making a request.

00:25:44.514 --> 00:25:48.594
Gimme the next 10 seconds, where
next is known to the server.

00:25:49.855 --> 00:25:55.014
Okay, so here the definition is not 10
minutes to 10 minutes and 10 seconds.

00:25:55.014 --> 00:25:57.084
The definition here is
the next 10 seconds.

00:25:58.375 --> 00:26:02.274
The server is now going to take
this request, look at the user's

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:05.695
current pointer, and then decide
which 10 seconds to pull out.

00:26:06.895 --> 00:26:09.985
So in the first case, it'll take 10
minutes to 10 minutes, 10 seconds.

00:26:10.435 --> 00:26:14.665
In the second case, it's going
to make a decision to send you

00:26:14.665 --> 00:26:18.774
the video from 10 minutes, 10
seconds to 10 minutes, 20 seconds.

00:26:19.855 --> 00:26:20.605
What's the benefit?

00:26:20.814 --> 00:26:25.554
The client didn't need to know which
part of the video it wants to watch.

00:26:26.139 --> 00:26:27.429
, it lets the server handle it.

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:32.980
It makes the overall network more
efficient because the client is not

00:26:32.980 --> 00:26:36.730
making duplicate requests, and it makes
the client code a little more simple.

00:26:37.149 --> 00:26:40.870
You don't need to define everything
every single time you let the

00:26:40.870 --> 00:26:43.449
server figure it out with context.

00:26:43.810 --> 00:26:46.810
That's a difference between a
stateful and a stateless protocol.

00:26:47.139 --> 00:26:48.790
HTP is stateless.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.909
Its benefit is that the
server is kept simple.

00:26:51.939 --> 00:26:55.389
If the server crashes, there is no
context on memory that is lost in

00:26:55.389 --> 00:26:56.126
the server.

00:26:56.126 --> 00:26:57.230
If you forget

00:26:57.280 --> 00:27:00.490
where the client currently was
pointing to, then this is a serious

00:27:00.495 --> 00:27:01.297
problem, right?

00:27:01.297 --> 00:27:02.500
You can go

00:27:02.500 --> 00:27:05.919
and store it in your database
instead and make a service stateless.

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:07.540
Therefore, the protocol that you're going

00:27:07.540 --> 00:27:11.180
to use htp can be stateless.

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:13.540
The best part about this is
you can add new servers without

00:27:13.540 --> 00:27:14.322
any issue.

00:27:14.322 --> 00:27:15.495
You have no

00:27:15.495 --> 00:27:16.689
state being stored in server.

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:17.620
So whenever a new

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:19.810
server pops up a new request

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:23.290
comes with total context in the
request itself, which you use.

00:27:24.159 --> 00:27:25.659
To query your data, right?

