Maybe you want to compress
it here in the next step.

00:38:31.480 --> 00:38:31.990
That can be in

00:38:31.990 --> 00:38:32.800
step two.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:33.610
The servers

00:38:33.610 --> 00:38:36.670
being used here might
be s3, S two and S one.

00:38:36.670 --> 00:38:38.650
So you see S one is
being used here and here.

00:38:39.250 --> 00:38:40.600
S3 is being used here and here.

00:38:40.930 --> 00:38:41.260
So

00:38:41.260 --> 00:38:43.300
there is no there is

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:48.520
no guaranteed execution server that
you have for one part of the process.

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:49.840
Any server, which is free.

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:50.920
We'll pick

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:53.230
up a task and execute

00:38:53.230 --> 00:38:55.660
it, whether it's compression
or transformation.

00:38:56.860 --> 00:39:01.090
Again, finally you get three
different outputs, which you

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:02.320
can store in your database.

00:39:04.120 --> 00:39:05.830
So this would be the map reduced pattern.

00:39:06.460 --> 00:39:09.010
It's a very high level overview of this.

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:11.260
It's almost a ca of the map
reduced pattern cause there's

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:14.085
no reduce in this process over here.

00:39:14.085 --> 00:39:15.700
But have a look

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:16.420
at this design pattern.

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:20.500
It is useful when it comes to
taking a single piece of data or

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:24.730
a data lake and converting it into
the data streams that you need.

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:28.420
Next, how is this data actually
going to go to the users

00:39:28.420 --> 00:39:30.100
who are looking to view it?

00:39:30.610 --> 00:39:33.520
So this data has to go over here to

00:39:33.525 --> 00:39:34.910
the server which is

00:39:34.960 --> 00:39:38.200
exposing these APIs of get put post.

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:42.190
When you query this data using a
protocol, you should be able to get it.

00:39:42.580 --> 00:39:45.910
We spoke about web RTC earlier, which
is a peer-to-peer protocol and it's

00:39:45.910 --> 00:39:47.620
really good for video conferences.

00:39:48.730 --> 00:39:51.550
Because multiple people are
streaming video together.

00:39:52.330 --> 00:39:56.110
However, in this case, it's a
broadcast, not a conference.

00:39:56.440 --> 00:40:00.310
So we can take a step back and say,
instead of web rtc, we are going to use

00:40:00.370 --> 00:40:02.830
a protocol which is more suitable for

00:40:02.830 --> 00:40:03.930
streaming.

00:40:03.930 --> 00:40:06.130
There's a

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:06.910
couple of protocols here.

00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:07.410
Also.

00:40:08.290 --> 00:40:14.080
The most popular one would be EG
dash, EG is a popular protocol.

00:40:14.530 --> 00:40:19.270
DASHER stands for Dynamic Adaptive
Streaming or sstp, which means

00:40:19.270 --> 00:40:22.930
that depending on your bandwidth,
depending on the network that

00:40:22.930 --> 00:40:24.195
you're on you are going

00:40:24.195 --> 00:40:27.220
to be able to see high quality
video or low quality video.

00:40:27.430 --> 00:40:29.770
For
example, you are going

00:40:29.770 --> 00:40:33.850
in a car, you're watching the video
at 10 adp, you enter a tunnel, your

00:40:33.850 --> 00:40:35.290
network is really poor.

00:40:35.320 --> 00:40:39.670
So you start watching it at 1 44 p as
a client, you don't want to handle it.

00:40:39.670 --> 00:40:41.350
You want the network
protocol to handle it.

00:40:42.130 --> 00:40:45.170
It's
defined in impact dash.

00:40:45.220 --> 00:40:46.660
Very similar protocol is hls.

00:40:46.660 --> 00:40:50.080
This is
useful for iOS or MAC

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:50.710
devices.

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:51.310
Okay.

00:40:51.310 --> 00:40:54.460
And finally, what kind of data do
you want to store on the server?

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:55.870
Do you want to store any data at all?

00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:57.220
Do you wanna make it totally stateless?

00:40:57.610 --> 00:41:00.310
Yes, statelessness is useful
when it comes to request

00:41:00.310 --> 00:41:02.590
serving and keeping context

00:41:02.590 --> 00:41:03.580
for every user.

00:41:03.910 --> 00:41:06.970
But for some things you can keep some

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:08.890
state when it comes

00:41:08.890 --> 00:41:13.660
to video, you can cash the last 10
minutes of video on your server.

00:41:14.140 --> 00:41:15.940
So anybody asking for video in

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:17.500
the last, that video,

00:41:17.500 --> 00:41:20.560
which is inside the last 10 minutes,
is going to get it from the cash.

00:41:20.590 --> 00:41:24.250
Instead of you making a full network call
to the database all the way over here,

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:27.070
you instead server from server itself.

00:41:27.160 --> 00:41:30.520
So you avoid this network call
saving time and bandwidth, both.

00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:32.950
So these are the high level
considerations that you have

00:41:32.950 --> 00:41:34.870
when it comes to a system design.

00:41:35.140 --> 00:41:38.230
Like we said, this is a large
scale distributed system.

00:41:38.350 --> 00:41:41.920
Our assumption is that you have
a lot of users because of which.

00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:43.780
This much planning and this kind

00:41:43.780 --> 00:41:45.460
of a design makes sense

00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:46.870
cost-wise and engineering

00:41:46.870 --> 00:41:47.845
effort wise.

00:41:47.845 --> 00:41:48.820
We mentioned

00:41:48.820 --> 00:41:50.620
that there needs to be
fall tolerance here.

00:41:50.625 --> 00:41:51.880
There needs to be performance here.

00:41:52.090 --> 00:41:55.030
So you can use things like CDN solutions.

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:59.740
So you can use content delivery
networks to persist some

00:41:59.740 --> 00:42:01.390
static data and have

00:42:01.390 --> 00:42:03.820
the clients actually pull
the starting data from here.

00:42:04.480 --> 00:42:05.860
Webpages are a good idea.

00:42:05.860 --> 00:42:06.670
You can also have some

00:42:06.670 --> 00:42:08.355
video data posted on

00:42:08.355 --> 00:42:12.130
the CDNs, in which case authentication
becomes a bit of a challenge because you

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:13.300
don't want everyone to be able to see

00:42:13.300 --> 00:42:14.238
this data.

00:42:14.238 --> 00:42:15.645
And does the

00:42:15.645 --> 00:42:17.080
CDN do the authentication for you?

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:21.310
Do you write some code and then
host it on the cdn, which does the

00:42:21.310 --> 00:42:21.800
authentication?

00:42:21.800 --> 00:42:22.780
These are

00:42:22.780 --> 00:42:25.210
the challenges that you have,
especially when you are designing

00:42:25.210 --> 00:42:27.970
a large scale distributed
system where performance is key.

00:42:28.510 --> 00:42:30.585
Okay,
so this is at a high

00:42:30.585 --> 00:42:34.870
level the things we need to consider when
we are designing any sort of a system.

00:42:35.305 --> 00:42:36.715
Where the requirements are defined.

00:42:36.715 --> 00:42:38.935
Maybe in the product requirement
document, maybe it's a

00:42:38.935 --> 00:42:39.436
startup.

00:42:39.436 --> 00:42:42.445
It's at a very, nascent stage.

00:42:42.445 --> 00:42:43.855
There is no requirement document.

00:42:43.855 --> 00:42:46.795
You're directly talking to the
customers and coming up with a

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:49.585
technological solution, which is
going to satisfy the requirements.

00:42:49.945 --> 00:42:55.225
The important things to notice are
that you define the requirements

00:42:55.225 --> 00:42:57.175
as abstract concepts like

00:42:57.175 --> 00:42:57.825
objects.

00:42:57.825 --> 00:42:59.125
These objects

00:42:59.125 --> 00:43:04.705
then need to be able to be manipulated
and queried using APIs on your server.

00:43:05.395 --> 00:43:08.845
The data representations need
to be stored in databases.

00:43:09.685 --> 00:43:14.125
Once this high level blueprint is done,
we start thinking about what exactly do

00:43:14.125 --> 00:43:16.965
we need to make this system possible?

00:43:17.125 --> 00:43:18.835
So what protocols are we going to use?

00:43:18.895 --> 00:43:20.575
What kind of database
solutions can we use?

00:43:20.575 --> 00:43:22.405
In some cases, you also think of what.

00:43:23.725 --> 00:43:26.845
Intermediate design pattern solutions
you can use, like you have load

00:43:26.845 --> 00:43:28.315
balances, you have message queues.

00:43:28.405 --> 00:43:29.455
These are all design

00:43:29.455 --> 00:43:30.045
patterns.

00:43:30.045 --> 00:43:31.225
And these

00:43:31.225 --> 00:43:34.075
have been converted into
tools by various companies.

00:43:34.105 --> 00:43:34.915
Redis, for example,

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:37.475
provides cas load balances

00:43:37.525 --> 00:43:40.135
and many other things are also
provided by cloud solution providers.

00:43:40.335 --> 00:43:46.915
Aws, you want to use these already
well-built, well-tested solutions

00:43:47.065 --> 00:43:49.315
instead of rebuilding it yourself unless

00:43:49.315 --> 00:43:50.815
you know the trade

00:43:50.815 --> 00:43:53.845
off in terms of cost and performance
is significant and it's worth it.

00:43:54.235 --> 00:43:57.685
Finally, once you have decided on the
tools, you think about the interactions

00:43:57.685 --> 00:44:01.735
of these tools and the interactions of
these services to meet your requirements.

00:44:02.155 --> 00:44:06.565
So we thought about how are we going
to take a video and show it to the

00:44:06.565 --> 00:44:06.929
user?

00:44:06.929 --> 00:44:08.384
Oh, we have to

00:44:08.435 --> 00:44:08.935
transform that video.

00:44:09.445 --> 00:44:13.475
We have to convert it into different
formats, otherwise it won't be visible

00:44:13.480 --> 00:44:14.785
in different operating systems.

00:44:15.235 --> 00:44:18.625
We then thought about how we are going
to stream this video to the server.

00:44:19.225 --> 00:44:21.355
We then thought about how we
are going to stream this video.

00:44:21.850 --> 00:44:22.930
To the client device.

00:44:23.380 --> 00:44:27.940
And finally, we are touched upon actually
streaming the video through a CDN solution

00:44:27.945 --> 00:44:31.780
instead of from your server, because
maybe your server is in the us and even

00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:33.460
if it is, close to the

00:44:33.460 --> 00:44:37.870
person who's streaming, you don't want
your server to handle all that load, all

00:44:37.875 --> 00:44:39.790
of that load all for some static content.

00:44:39.790 --> 00:44:44.500
You want to be giving that load
away to well-known solutions like

00:44:44.500 --> 00:44:47.350
content delivery networks, which
can be tied up with their ISPs.

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:49.300
You can have a look at
how Netflix does it here.

00:44:51.670 --> 00:44:54.130
Another part of system
design is low level design.

00:44:54.610 --> 00:44:57.850
This is in contrast with what
we just saw, which is high

00:44:57.855 --> 00:44:59.590
level design where we

00:44:59.590 --> 00:45:04.030
took different components of the
entire system and thought about how

00:45:04.030 --> 00:45:07.570
those components are going to interact
with each other using network calls,

00:45:07.640 --> 00:45:08.728
using APIs.

00:45:08.728 --> 00:45:10.360
And then you're

00:45:10.360 --> 00:45:12.700
going to be sending data
from one place to another.

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:15.190
But largely if you look at these

00:45:15.195 --> 00:45:19.090
systems, they own the business

00:45:19.090 --> 00:45:20.860
data related to that service.

00:45:21.205 --> 00:45:25.045
Now what we are going to do
is take certain functions of

00:45:25.045 --> 00:45:26.915
these services and try to

00:45:26.965 --> 00:45:30.175
code it out, which means we
are going to go into much

00:45:30.175 --> 00:45:31.407
more depth.

00:45:31.407 --> 00:45:32.640
But because

00:45:32.640 --> 00:45:34.255
of that, we are probably
going lose some breadth.

00:45:35.005 --> 00:45:35.215
Okay?

00:45:35.215 --> 00:45:38.065
When you, at the high design
of a system, you are looking at

00:45:38.065 --> 00:45:39.565
everything from how the users will be

00:45:39.565 --> 00:45:41.775
interacting with your gateway.

00:45:41.815 --> 00:45:43.885
The gateway is going to be
actually sending these requests

00:45:43.890 --> 00:45:45.355
to your internal services.

00:45:45.475 --> 00:45:47.515
The internal services
are looking at databases.

00:45:47.575 --> 00:45:48.505
You can't code all of

00:45:48.505 --> 00:45:49.449
this out.

00:45:49.449 --> 00:45:51.337
Even in, at work.

00:45:51.337 --> 00:45:51.810
You

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:54.055
can't just have this all
in one document, one page.

00:45:54.295 --> 00:45:59.395
So you take small chunks of the system
and you try to elaborate on each chunk.

00:46:00.325 --> 00:46:01.615
That would be the Louis design.

00:46:02.065 --> 00:46:08.545
The core functionality of live streaming
is to be able to view a video as a

00:46:08.545 --> 00:46:10.255
customer, as a person who's paid.

00:46:10.555 --> 00:46:12.265
Maybe it's a subscription,
maybe it's a one-time

00:46:12.270 --> 00:46:12.587
purchase.

00:46:12.587 --> 00:46:13.855
But what we are

00:46:13.855 --> 00:46:18.325
looking at here is how
does a user fetch video?

00:46:18.775 --> 00:46:24.415
View video and fetch more of the video
and continue doing so, video ends, right?

00:46:24.415 --> 00:46:27.715
We are not looking at onboarding
the video, onboarding a

00:46:27.715 --> 00:46:30.390
movie or how the livestream

00:46:30.390 --> 00:46:33.745
is going to be moved from the
source camera to our system.

00:46:34.105 --> 00:46:37.195
We are looking on the other side,
the user side of how it's going

00:46:37.225 --> 00:46:38.875
to be consumed by the users.

00:46:38.904 --> 00:46:41.154
To do this, we again, have two approaches.

00:46:41.515 --> 00:46:44.875
One is to think of the code in the start.

00:46:45.265 --> 00:46:49.195
So if you are an object oriented
programming language person,

00:46:49.195 --> 00:46:52.345
you might think of what kind
of objects do I need to have in

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:53.058
my system?

00:46:53.058 --> 00:46:54.120
How are these

00:46:54.120 --> 00:46:55.165
objects to interact with

00:46:55.165 --> 00:46:55.717
each other?

00:46:55.717 --> 00:46:56.545
Is there any

00:46:56.545 --> 00:46:59.095
kind of inheritance I have
to take into consideration?

00:46:59.395 --> 00:47:03.805
But as earlier, this is a little difficult
to think of unless the requirements

00:47:03.810 --> 00:47:08.154
are extremely well specified or they're
so generic that you have to look at

00:47:08.154 --> 00:47:09.235
the data that you're storing for.

00:47:09.355 --> 00:47:13.495
Before you think of the function, I
would suggest you think of the user.

00:47:13.855 --> 00:47:15.695
So even over here, we think about.

00:47:16.735 --> 00:47:19.585
, what are the actions
that a user can perform?

00:47:20.995 --> 00:47:25.885
So what I'll be doing is on my phone
or on my desktop, I'll be scrolling to

00:47:25.885 --> 00:47:28.465
a particular place, watch the video.

00:47:29.365 --> 00:47:29.550
Okay?

00:47:29.550 --> 00:47:32.305
So I'll be scrub the video up to a
particular point that can happen.

00:47:32.575 --> 00:47:34.375
I can also click on play from the start.

00:47:34.380 --> 00:47:36.865
So start the video at the beginning at

00:47:36.865 --> 00:47:37.945
timestamp zero.

00:47:37.945 --> 00:47:39.565
Both of these

00:47:39.565 --> 00:47:40.555
are very close to each other.

00:47:40.765 --> 00:47:41.275
So

00:47:41.585 --> 00:47:43.105
effectively the action

00:47:43.105 --> 00:47:47.665
is play video at timestamp X
under the functionality that

00:47:47.665 --> 00:47:49.465
I need is to pause the video.

00:47:49.975 --> 00:47:50.425
Okay.

00:47:50.425 --> 00:47:51.475
In which case, what do you do?

00:47:52.615 --> 00:47:56.305
Do you continue fetching more
segments from the backend or

00:47:56.305 --> 00:47:58.615
do you stop touching segments?

00:47:59.395 --> 00:48:02.875
So the behavior is dependent on the
user experience that you want to give.

00:48:03.055 --> 00:48:06.665
If you don't wanna clog up their mobile
with the entire video, while they've just

00:48:06.665 --> 00:48:08.935
kept it, in the background then you

00:48:08.985 --> 00:48:09.745
have to be smart about it.

00:48:09.805 --> 00:48:14.515
Probably the next 20 or 30 seconds or
one or two minutes can be buffered.

00:48:15.370 --> 00:48:16.390
into your device.

00:48:16.750 --> 00:48:20.259
So that behavior also has to be
coded inside, despite pausing.

00:48:20.470 --> 00:48:25.000
We go for the next two minutes of video
from the place that you have seated

00:48:25.060 --> 00:48:30.910
right now under the important requirement
is that depending on your device, the

00:48:30.910 --> 00:48:35.020
video quality, which is going to be
fetched, is going to be either hd.

00:48:35.080 --> 00:48:39.100
Let's say you are using
another desktop or for atp,

00:48:39.200 --> 00:48:41.170
let's say for low resolution

00:48:41.170 --> 00:48:46.060
devices, and one final feature is up
to what point have you played a video?

00:48:46.360 --> 00:48:50.259
Which means if you had a one hour long
video, let's say there's a cricket match

00:48:50.259 --> 00:48:54.340
between India and Pakistan, and it's
one hour long and you are seen up to

00:48:54.340 --> 00:48:59.680
the 20th minute, if you log out and come
back and want to watch the same video,

00:48:59.950 --> 00:49:01.990
we should start at the 20th minute.

00:49:02.140 --> 00:49:05.020
We should store that somewhere so
that you have a good user experience.

00:49:05.740 --> 00:49:09.430
You might want to cash the video like we
were doing earlier, the next two minutes.

00:49:09.430 --> 00:49:10.060
You want to hit that

00:49:10.060 --> 00:49:11.470
buffer so that you start

00:49:11.470 --> 00:49:14.259
playing immediately when the user
has come, or you may not want to

00:49:14.265 --> 00:49:15.027
do that.

00:49:15.027 --> 00:49:15.790
You might

00:49:15.790 --> 00:49:18.610
want to do that just for some videos,
which have been recently watched.

00:49:18.640 --> 00:49:20.960
The older videos then can be kicked outta

00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:21.705
the cash.

00:49:21.705 --> 00:49:22.450
That What

00:49:22.450 --> 00:49:27.040
I'm considering here are
memory optimizations, user

00:49:27.040 --> 00:49:28.930
behavior, and API calling.

00:49:29.680 --> 00:49:32.890
This is largely what lu design
will mean when it comes to

00:49:32.920 --> 00:49:34.180
interacting with services.

00:49:34.509 --> 00:49:38.020
Depending on your level of seniority,
and depending on the use case, you might

00:49:38.020 --> 00:49:43.480
have issues of concurrency, latency
and throughput also come in here.

00:49:44.140 --> 00:49:47.740
An example use case will be
the workflow that we had for

00:49:47.860 --> 00:49:50.650
chunking and transforming videos.

00:49:50.890 --> 00:49:51.610
I'll leave that as an

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:52.684
exercise to you.

00:49:52.684 --> 00:49:53.380
But there

00:49:53.380 --> 00:49:57.850
are cases where you want to increase
your throughput to the maximum, so

00:49:57.910 --> 00:49:59.290
you don't really care that much about

00:49:59.290 --> 00:49:59.930
latency.

00:49:59.930 --> 00:50:01.210
How quickly

00:50:01.210 --> 00:50:02.680
do you get those videos
through the pipeline?

00:50:02.680 --> 00:50:04.060
That's not your concern.

00:50:04.299 --> 00:50:07.150
You want to make sure that
your pipeline is continuously

00:50:07.150 --> 00:50:08.575
functioning right.

00:50:08.575 --> 00:50:10.000
Another cases

00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:13.390
where the moment a video
comes in,  the new video.

00:50:13.450 --> 00:50:16.180
You want to respond to it as
quickly as possible with the

00:50:16.180 --> 00:50:17.230
assumption that video

00:50:17.230 --> 00:50:18.130
is really important.

00:50:18.580 --> 00:50:20.890
So there's going to be some sort
of context switching over there.

00:50:21.100 --> 00:50:21.850
Okay.
I'll leave that as

